# Functional test: the out-of-SA error notification, over a real UDP socket.
#
# One unprivileged ze instance runs an IKEv2 responder on 127.0.0.1. A native Go fixture probe
# speaks to its real IKE socket and proves three things RFC 7296 Section 2.21.4
# requires, none of which any unit test can prove through the socket:
#
#   1. A datagram naming an IKE SA ze does not hold draws an unprotected
#      INVALID_IKE_SPI response that copies both SPIs, the Exchange Type and the
#      Message ID, and sets the Response flag.
#   2. That response, fed straight back to the same socket, draws NOTHING. The
#      emitter is a fixed point, so two ze instances cannot exchange error
#      notifications without end. This is the property a unit test argues and only a
#      live socket demonstrates.
#   3. A datagram already marked as a response draws nothing at all.
#
# The probe asserts an ABSENCE by ordering, never by a clock: after the datagram that
# must draw nothing it sends a second datagram that MUST draw a reply, and requires
# that reply to be the next thing it reads. One socket keeps datagram order on
# loopback, so a reply that arrived first would be read first.
#
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-1 positive -- a datagram marked as a response draws
# nothing from the real socket, and the ordering marker that follows it proves the
# silence rather than a timeout.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-1 negative -- the same datagram with the Response flag
# clear does draw INVALID_IKE_SPI, so the silence comes from the guard.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-2 positive -- the answer arrives on the socket the
# request came from and copies both SPIs, the Exchange Type and a NON-ZERO Message ID.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-2 negative -- a second request carrying a different
# initiator SPI draws an answer carrying THAT SPI, so the fields are copied per request.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-4 positive -- the answer holds one Notify payload of
# type INVALID_IKE_SPI, with an empty SPI field and no Notification Data.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-4 negative -- an IKE_SA_INIT to the same unknown SPI
# pair draws no payload at all, so the Notify depends on the emitter running.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-5 positive -- ze's own answer, fed straight back into
# the same socket, draws NOTHING. This is the no-loop property through the real UDP
# path, where a mistake would actually run away between two daemons.
# RFC requirement: RFC7296-2.21.4-5 negative -- the ordering marker sent right after it
# DOES draw an answer, so the fixed point is not silence from a dead socket.

option=env:var=ze_test_ike_port:value=$PORT2
# noop dataplane: unprivileged XFRM install is EPERM-fatal by design.
option=env:var=ze_test_ike_dataplane:value=noop
option=timeout:value=45s


# Responder daemon: binds 127.0.0.1 and holds no SA with the probe's SPIs.
stdin=responder:terminator=EOF_RESPONDER
vpn {
	ipsec {
		esp-group esp-default {
			lifetime 3600
			proposal 1 {
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		ike-group ike-default {
			key-exchange ikev2
			lifetime 28800
			proposal 1 {
				dh-group 14
				encryption aes256
				hash sha256
			}
		}
		site-to-site {
			peer initiator {
				ike-group ike-default
				esp-group esp-default
				connection-type respond
				local-address 127.0.0.1
				remote-address 127.0.0.2
				authentication {
					mode pre-shared-secret
					pre-shared-secret test-key-1234
				}
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_RESPONDER

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=responder
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture ipsec/error-notify-no-loop:timeout=30s

expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=probe: all out-of-SA notification assertions passed
reject=stderr:pattern=FAIL:
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
